From 7152a86c5819add432a222da234ffcc5c80cc63c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David Schachter <davidschachter@appdevforall.org>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 11:47:56 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 07/10] Keep the JavaScript stack size within the thread's
 stack

QuickJS stops code that calls itself too deeply when the stack falls
below a limit it computes from the size it was given. It trusts that
size, so a size larger than the thread's stack never trips the check,
and code that calls itself without end crashes the process instead of
failing with "Maximum call stack size exceeded".

js_set_max_stack_size() passed its argument to JS_SetMaxStackSize()
unchecked, through sqlite3_value_int(): text such as '1MB' or 'abc'
read as 1 or 0, and a size above the thread's stack, such as 16 MB on
a thread with 8 MB, crashed sqlite3 on endless recursion. The 1 MB
default could itself be too large: on a thread with 512 KB of stack,
endless recursion crashed at the default, and Windows gives a main
thread 1 MB, apps often their threads no more.

Measure the calling thread's stack (pthread_getattr_np() on Linux and
Android, pthread_get_stackaddr_np() and pthread_get_stacksize_np() on
Apple platforms, VirtualQuery() on Windows) and keep a margin,
SQLITE_JS_STACK_MARGIN (256 KB), for the native code that runs between
two of QuickJS's checks, such as SQLite when JavaScript runs SQL
through db.exec. js_set_max_stack_size() now accepts an integer up to
what the stack allows, and reports the largest size in its error
message; elsewhere it allows up to the default. When the extension is
loaded, the default is lowered to what the loading thread's stack
allows. The stack position is read the way QuickJS reads it, because
under AddressSanitizer a local variable lives on a separate stack.

0 still turns the check off, as the 1.3.7 changelog documents for a
connection used from several threads, and test_thread relies on.

Add a Stack size test: endless recursion fails at the default, invalid
sizes are errors, and endless recursion, also through db.exec, fails
cleanly just below the largest size allowed and at a smaller one. It
fails without this change.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Vh2DAXGyHbMzjSsi2zbrkw
